      "3_4": "Everything around us is made of particles, which are tiny units of matter that are always moving. This idea explains why you can smell cookies from across a room. Smell particles spread through the room by diffusion, which means particles spread from crowded areas to less crowded areas.\n\nThermal energy is the energy from the motion of particles. With more thermal energy, particles move faster. If matter gains enough thermal energy, it can go through a phase change. A phase change is matter changing between solid, liquid, and gas, such as ice melting into water. Scientists use particle models like these to explain smells and melting.",
      "5_6": "Everything around us is made of particles, tiny units of matter that are in constant motion. This idea helps explain why you can smell cookies from across a room. Smell particles spread through the room by diffusion, a process in which particles move from crowded areas to less crowded areas until they are evenly spread out.\n\nThermal energy is the energy that comes from the motion of particles. With more thermal energy, particles move faster. If matter gains or loses enough thermal energy, it can undergo a phase change, which is matter changing between solid, liquid, and gas. For example, ice melts into liquid water when its particles gain enough energy to move more freely. Scientists use particle models like these to explain smells, melting, and many other everyday events. In every case, a model of moving particles connects what we observe to what we cannot see. Thermal energy and particle motion therefore link smells, temperature, and changes of state.",
