      "1_2": "The Great Lakes are home to many fish. Living things are linked in a food web.\n\nThe sea lamprey is an invasive species. It does not belong there. A sea lamprey is a predator. A predator hunts other animals for food. It attaches to big fish like lake trout. It feeds on them.\n\nThe lake trout population gets smaller. A population is all members of one species in an area. Fish also face competition. Competition is a struggle for the same resource.",
      "3_4": "The Great Lakes have many fish, and living things there are linked in a food web. The sea lamprey is an invasive species, which means it is a non-native organism that disrupts an ecosystem. It is also a predator, an animal that hunts other animals for food. It attaches to big fish like lake trout and feeds on them, so the lake trout population became smaller. A population is all the members of one species in an area.\n\nFish in the lakes also face competition, which happens when organisms struggle for the same limited resource, such as food. Scientists use population graphs and food webs to build arguments about what happened.",
      "5_6": "The Great Lakes support many fish, and the living things there are connected in food webs. The sea lamprey is an invasive species, a non-native organism that disrupts an ecosystem. It is also a predator, an animal that hunts other animals for food. The lamprey attaches to big fish like lake trout and feeds on them, so the lake trout population became much smaller. A population is all the members of one species in an area.\n\nFish in the lakes also face competition, which happens when organisms struggle for the same limited resource, such as food. When one species declines and another changes, the whole food web can shift. Scientists therefore study food webs and population graphs, and they use the data to build careful arguments about what happened to the creatures of the Great Lakes. Understanding these connections helps communities protect native fish and manage the lakes wisely. Evidence from long-term counts of fish makes these arguments stronger.",
