      "1_2": "Some of the first civilizations grew near rivers. Mesopotamia was between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers. Egypt and Kush were along the Nile River.\n\nFarmers used irrigation. Irrigation brings water to crops through canals. Farms grew more food, so cities grew.\n\nA civilization has cities, a government, and writing. People in Mesopotamia wrote in cuneiform. Cuneiform is wedge-shaped writing. Egypt had a dynasty. A dynasty is a line of rulers from one family.",
      "3_4": "Some of the first civilizations grew near rivers. Mesopotamia lay between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, while Egypt and Kush were along the Nile River.\n\nFarmers in these places used irrigation, which brings water to crops through canals. With irrigation, farms grew more food, so cities could grow.\n\nA civilization is a complex society with cities, government, and writing. People in Mesopotamia wrote in cuneiform, a wedge-shaped writing system. Egypt was ruled by a dynasty, which is a line of rulers from the same family. Rivers gave people water, rich soil, and a way to travel and trade. These resources helped societies grow.",
      "5_6": "Some of the earliest civilizations developed along rivers. Mesopotamia lay between the Tigris and Euphrates rivers, while Egypt and Kush grew along the Nile River. Each river provided water, fertile soil, and a route for travel and trade.\n\nFarmers in these regions used irrigation, which brings water to crops through canals. With irrigation, farms grew more food, so cities could support larger populations. A civilization is a complex society with cities, government, and writing.\n\nIn Mesopotamia, scribes wrote in cuneiform, a wedge-shaped writing system pressed into clay. Egypt was ruled by dynasties, and a dynasty is a line of rulers from the same family. Together, rivers, farming, government, and writing helped these societies grow. Each society was different, but all three depended on a river for survival. Historians compare them to understand how geography shaped government, writing, and trade. These comparisons show that location and resources influenced how early societies developed.",
