      "5_6": "The Silk Road was a vast trade network that linked China, India, and lands far to the west. Traders carried silk and spices, and they also carried ideas. This exchange is called cultural diffusion, which is the spread of ideas, art, and technology between societies. For example, Buddhism spread from India to China along these routes.\n\nIn China, a large empire united many territories under one supreme ruler. Thinkers such as Confucius shared a philosophy, a system of ideas about ethics and how to live, that shaped society.\n\nIn India, a monsoon is a seasonal wind that brings heavy rain each summer. Farmers depend on this rain to grow their crops, so the monsoon shaped daily life as much as trade routes and empires did. The Silk Road, empires, philosophies, and monsoon rains show how geography, power, and ideas shaped each other in the ancient world. Historians study these connections to understand how societies influenced one another across great distances.",
