      "3_4": "Athens had a democracy, a government in which citizens hold voting power. Citizens voted directly on laws. However, not everyone was a citizen. Only free adult men were citizens, and women, enslaved people, and foreigners were not citizens. Citizenship is legal membership with rights and duties.\n\nRome was a republic, a government where citizens elect leaders to speak for them. Roman citizens chose officials called consuls.\n\nHow do we know about these governments? Historians study a primary source, which is a firsthand historical document or artifact, such as a law or a letter. Comparing sources helps students understand who held power. Each government shows a different way to share power.",
      "5_6": "Athens developed a democracy, a government in which citizens hold voting power. Citizens voted directly on laws instead of leaving decisions to leaders. However, citizenship was limited. Only free adult men were citizens, and women, enslaved people, and foreigners were not citizens. Citizenship is legal membership in a community with rights and duties.\n\nRome developed a republic, a government in which citizens elect leaders to speak for them. Roman citizens chose officials called consuls, though many people in Rome also lacked full rights.\n\nHow do we know about these governments? Historians study a primary source, a firsthand historical document or artifact, such as a law, a speech, or a letter. By comparing sources, students can build arguments about who held power and who did not. Athens and Rome show that governments can give citizens power while still leaving many people out. Studying their rules helps students judge how fair each system was. Primary sources give the strongest evidence for these judgments.",
